INT. CRAB CANNERY - NIGHT SHIFT - NIGHT

Steel tables. Hoses. Steam rising from vats. Fluorescents buzz. Everything smells of brine and bleach.

PAULINA RUSSO, 40s, shift lead, hairnet, white rubber boots, sprays down a conveyor belt. DESMOND WALCOTT, 20s, headphones around his neck, stacks crates. GUS, 60s, Paulina's oldest hand, wheels a pallet jack.

The line is stopped. A quiet kind of peace.

PAULINA
(to all)
Ten minutes, everybody. Breaker's being cranky.

DESMOND
It's always cranky.

PAULINA
Then it's consistent.

Gus parks the jack. Eases onto an upturned bucket. Pulls a sandwich from his apron.

GUS
(to himself)
Thirty-one years. Never once got used to the cold.

Desmond laughs. Pulls a phone from his pocket.

DESMOND
Gus, you want to see my niece? She just learned to whistle.

GUS
Sure.

Desmond shows him the screen. Gus squints. Smiles. The first real smile of the night.

Paulina checks her clipboard. Hums. The steam curls around her.

PAULINA
(under her breath)
Two cases short. Where did you go, little guys.

From the live tank behind the line, a soft tap. Glass on glass.

Nobody notices. The humming continues.

DESMOND
She's three. She calls everything "doggy."

GUS
Smart kid.

Tap. Tap. Tap. Slower.

Paulina looks up. The tank, a long glass rectangle of dark water, hides in the shadow beyond the last light.

PAULINA
(to Desmond)
Did you top up the tank?

DESMOND
Friday.

PAULINA
Then who's tapping?

DESMOND
Pipes. They knock.

Gus has stopped chewing. He stares at the tank.

GUS
(quietly)
Paulina.

PAULINA
What?

GUS
There's no water moving.

She follows his gaze. The surface is perfectly flat. Black. Nothing disturbs it.

Tap.

The sound comes from inside.

PAULINA
(to herself)
Okay. Okay.

She walks toward the tank. Boots squelching. Desmond rises. Gus doesn't.

PAULINA (CONT'D)
(over her shoulder)
Both of you, stay there.

She reaches the glass. Wipes the condensation with her sleeve.

Pressed against the inside of the glass is a HAND. A human hand. Pale, wrinkled, fingers splayed.

Paulina stumbles back. Her clipboard clatters.

Under the surface, a face drifts up. Eyes open. Wearing a hairnet.

It's Gus's face.

Paulina turns. Gus is still on his bucket. He lifts his sandwich. Smiles. Too wide.

GUS
Ten minutes, you said.

Desmond's phone slips from his hand. The screen lights the floor. The breaker clunks.

All the fluorescents go out.
